Sonic is a simple algorithm for speeding up or slowing down speech.  However,
it's optimized for speed ups of over 2X, unlike previous algorithms for changing
speech rate.  The Sonic library is a very simple ANSI C library that is designed
to easily be integrated into streaming voice applications, like TTS back ends.

The primary motivation behind Sonic is to enable the blind and visually impaired
to improve their productivity with open source speech engines, like espeak.
Sonic can also be used by the sighted.  For example, Sonic can improve the
experience of listening to an audio book on an Android phone.

A native Java port of Sonic is in Sonic.java.  Main.java is a simple example of
how to use Sonic.java.  To play with it, you'll need a "talking.wav" file in the
current directory, and you'll want to change the speed, pitch or other
parameters manually in Main.java, in the main method.

Sonic is Copyright 2010, 2011, Bill Cox, all rights reserved.  It is released
under the Apache 2.0 license, to promote usage as widely as possible.

Performance test:

I sped up a 751958176 byte wav file with sonic (a 9 hour, 28 minute mono audio
file encoded at 16-bit 11.KHz), but with the output writing disabled.  The
reported time, running Ubuntu 11.04 on my HP Pavilion dm4 laptop was:

real    0m50.839s
user    0m47.370s
sys     0m0.620s

The Java version is not much slower.  It reported:

real    0m52.043s
user    0m51.190s
sys     0m0.310s

Update, May 7, 2017
-------------------
I upgraded the pitch change algorithm to use a 12-point sinc FIR filter for
interpolation, rather than linearly interpolating between points.  This
significantly reduces noise introduced by the pitch change algorithm.  It is
most noticable in low-sample-rate streams, such as the 11,025 Hz output of the
Eloquence TTS engine.  The upgrade is in both the C and Java versions.


Author: Bill Cox
email: waywardgeek@gmail.com

Speed tracks
------------

`sonicSetSpeedTrack` applies a time-varying, pitch-preserving speed curve to a
stream.  A track is an array of `SonicSpeedPoint` values:

    typedef struct SonicSpeedPoint {
      uint64_t inputFrame;
      float speed;
    } SonicSpeedPoint;

`inputFrame` is a position in the original (source) PCM, measured in frames
(samples per channel, so stereo positions count frames, not interleaved
samples).  It does not depend on how the caller chunks writes.  `speed` is the
time-compression factor at that position: 1.0 is normal, 2.0 is twice as fast,
0.5 is half speed.  The speed is interpolated linearly between points, and the
last point's speed is held for the remainder of the stream.  For example, to
rush through a long pause and then ease back to normal speech:

    SonicSpeedPoint points[3] = {
      {0,        2.0f},   /* rush through the pause */
      {24000,    2.0f},   /* pause ends at source frame 24000 */
      {36000,    1.0f},   /* ease back to normal by frame 36000 */
    };
    sonicSetSpeedTrack(stream, points, 3);

Coordinates are source frames of an unsigned 64-bit position, but the format
limits positions to 2^53 so they can be represented exactly as a double.  The
first point must be at frame 0, positions must increase strictly, and every
speed must be finite and in [0.5, 2.0].

Setting restrictions:

* A track may only be set on a new stream that has not yet been given any
  non-empty input (`sonicWriteShortToStream` with a non-zero count, etc.).
  The call returns 1 on success and 0 if the track is rejected.  When it
  returns 0 the previous configuration is left untouched.
* Track mode currently supports pitch == rate == 1, quality == 1, and 16 kHz
  or 48 kHz with 1 or 2 channels of 16-bit PCM.
* While a track is active, `sonicSetSampleRate`, `sonicSetNumChannels`,
  `sonicSetPitch`, `sonicSetRate` and the regular `sonicSetSpeed` are ignored:
  the existing values are kept.  `sonicSetVolume` still takes effect.
* An empty track (`count == 0`, or a NULL points array) clears the mode and
  restores the regular behavior, after which all of the above setters work
  again.  Passing count == 0 is allowed regardless of the format limits.
* The library keeps its own copy of the points; the caller's array may be
  freed right after the call.

Behavior:

* The speed change is pitch-preserving (a 220 Hz tone stays at 220 Hz), and a
  node in the middle of an analysis window is handled continuously: pitch
  period history is not reset, no silence is inserted at node boundaries, and
  the output is not built by generating each segment separately and splicing
  them together.  A single write may cross several nodes.
* Segment duration follows the continuous speed curve: it is the integral of
  the inverse speed over the source frames, and it does not grow with the
  recording length.  The output may be read while writing is still in
  progress; the PCM buffered by the stream does not grow with the total
  recording length if reads are prompt.
* Trailing padding is used only to flush audio that has already been generated
  and is never counted as source coordinates.  The produced length is within
  two `maxRequired` frames of the duration integral (measured at the flush
  target), and padding only makes up the small shortfall left by the final
  partial analysis window; it never adds length in proportion to the recording.
* A track of all 1.0 speeds reproduces the input samples exactly.
* After `sonicFlushStream` the stream is reusable as usual; the next segment
  starts again at source frame 0 with the same track.  To use a different
  track (or change any of the restricted settings) for the next segment,
  clear the track with `sonicSetSpeedTrack(stream, NULL, 0)` first.

Tests: `tests/speed_track_test.c` exercises the API, including the independent
duration integral and steady-state frequency checks.  Run it (together with the
existing tests) with `bash task_support/target.sh` on a build that links
`sonic.c`.
